                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="726-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_o.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Omnidirectional" title="Omnidirectional" ><img id="726-1" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ><img id="726-2" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_8.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Bidirectional" title="Bidirectional" ></span>Audio-Technica AT2050</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Multi-Pattern Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The AT2050 is a medium-diaphragm multipattern FET condenser microphone with transformer-coupled output.</p><p>The capsule has two gold-sputtered diaphragms and is externally biased. Although described by A-T as a large-diaphragm design, the active diaphragm area measures just 20.4mm across. Backplate diameter is also shy of the traditional &#8220;large&#8221; cutoff, at 24.3mm.</p><p>The mic has unusually high SPL handling capabilities, spec&#8217;d at 149dB without the pad or 159dB with the pad engaged.</p><p>At 7.9mV/Pa, the AT2050 is much less sensitive than its single-pattern lookalike sibling, the AT2035 (22 mV/Pa), due to the need to accommodate the signal of the rear capsule in all modes but Cardioid.</p><p>A switch on the mic body allows selection of Cardioid, Omni, or Figure-of-8 pickup patterns.</p><p>The mic&#8217;s stated frequency range is 20-20,000Hz, but the frequency plots show a HF -3dB point closer to 16kHz.</p><p>The AT2050 ships with a shock mount (p/n AT8458) and a storage pouch.</p><blockquote><p><cite>ProAudioReview</cite></p><p>There also seems to be quite a bit more &#8220;midrange punch&#8221; present in the AT2050 compared to the <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/Audio-Technica/AT4050">4050</a> and <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/Neumann/U-87-Ai">U 87</a>, which I found to be helpful when putting the mic on a guitar cabinet. Those punchy qualities really helped me not need to reach for a midrange EQ boost in that upper-mid 3 kHz area.</p></blockquote><p><p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Audio-Technica/AT2050">Audio-Technica AT2050</a> is also known as: AT-2050.</p><p>The mic was released in 2008.</p>
